1
00:00:24,095 --> 00:00:31,527
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundations for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:31,547 --> 00:00:39,198
Now we are going to start a new topic, which is Optimizing VR Navigation Experience.

3
00:00:41,883 --> 00:00:46,069
Before we start, let us quickly recap what we have learned so far.

4
00:00:49,019 --> 00:01:01,881
In our learning so far, we have mostly focused on the foundational aspects of extended reality systems, which included the idea of VR systems,

5
00:01:01,881 --> 00:01:16,242
namely the hardware and software used to build such systems, and also extended reality systems in general, including AR systems,

6
00:01:20,187 --> 00:01:32,585
the role of human factors, how they are important, why we should bother about them, and how they can be taken into account while building extended reality systems.

7
00:01:32,605 --> 00:01:37,633
We have also learned about the design lifecycle for any extended reality system.

8
00:01:37,653 --> 00:01:39,515
There we talked about interactive systems.

9
00:01:39,836 --> 00:01:43,722
Extended reality systems are essentially a subset of interactive systems.

10
00:01:44,222 --> 00:01:47,087
And we talked about the interactive system development lifecycle.

11
00:01:47,447 --> 00:01:48,709
Primarily we focused on

12
00:01:49,094 --> 00:02:00,950
the requirement gathering, the design-prototype-evaluate cycle, that is, interface and interaction design, where we learned about design guidelines, prototyping, and expert evaluation techniques.

13
00:02:01,751 --> 00:02:10,683
Also, we talked about implementation and usability evaluation of such systems.

14
00:02:14,127 --> 00:02:16,350
Now, all these concepts

15
00:02:17,309 --> 00:02:19,832
pertain to usable system development.

16
00:02:19,872 --> 00:02:30,290
So, essentially, our objective is to build systems that are usable, and in order to do so, whatever we needed to know, whatever we needed to use,

17
00:02:30,290 --> 00:02:38,693
all those basic things we have covered so far, with the objective that we should be able to enhance the user experience.

18
00:02:40,976 --> 00:02:42,878
Now, the considerations that we have

19
00:02:43,668 --> 00:02:54,004
covered so far, the topics that we have covered so far, are important considerations, no doubt.

20
00:02:54,024 --> 00:03:01,616
But there are more such important considerations to be taken into account while building systems.

21
00:03:01,636 --> 00:03:07,525
So, usability is something that rests on many things.

22
00:03:07,986 --> 00:03:12,072
We have covered a few of those, and there are many other such considerations,

23
00:03:13,013 --> 00:03:18,785
when we are talking of usable system development and deployment.

24
00:03:18,805 --> 00:03:25,540
Now, other considerations can broadly be related to the issues of optimization.

25
00:03:26,402 --> 00:03:32,575
So, we are going to devote time to understanding what these issues of optimization are.

26
00:03:39,271 --> 00:03:48,584
Here, in these lectures, we will primarily focus on optimization in VR, immersive experience in particular.

27
00:03:49,205 --> 00:03:54,833
Now, we can look at this optimization problem, the issues of optimization, from two perspectives.

28
00:03:56,856 --> 00:04:02,103
One is optimized experience, that is, psychophysiological experience optimization.

29
00:04:03,005 --> 00:04:08,232
The other one is optimized resource utilization or resource requirement, that is,

30
00:04:08,566 --> 00:04:09,967
technological optimization.

31
00:04:11,008 --> 00:04:16,333
So, these two perspectives we can think of while talking about optimization in VR.

32
00:04:19,897 --> 00:04:34,410
These two optimization requirements are there in any XR system, of course, but the thing is that in an immersive experience, the optimization requirements gain more prominence.

33
00:04:34,430 --> 00:04:37,493
So, we will be focusing mostly on immersive VR experience,

34
00:04:37,979 --> 00:04:42,065
and immersive VR systems while dealing with optimization issues.

35
00:04:43,567 --> 00:04:45,831
So, let us first talk about experience optimization.

36
00:04:47,293 --> 00:04:53,883
Now, earlier we talked about a major issue in immersive VR, that is the issue of cyber sickness.

37
00:04:53,903 --> 00:05:04,931
When we talk about an optimized experience, one way to look at it is to design the experience so as to minimize cyber sickness.

38
00:05:04,931 --> 00:05:17,933
So, when we are trying to minimize cyber sickness or minimize the possibility of the induction of cyber sickness while using a system, then we can roughly call it an experience optimization problem.

39
00:05:23,419 --> 00:05:24,160
What is the other one?

40
00:05:24,381 --> 00:05:25,442
Resource optimization.

41
00:05:25,882 --> 00:05:29,907
So, in XR or extended reality in general and VR in particular.

42
00:05:33,295 --> 00:05:35,318
The systems are generally resource-hungry.

43
00:05:38,382 --> 00:05:47,567
To get some idea of what this means, you can recap our discussion on the VR hardware and software where we talked about the hardware requirements,

44
00:05:47,567 --> 00:05:56,548
what kind of hardware we need to suitably deploy VR systems in particular.

45
00:05:58,671 --> 00:06:03,117
Now, if the requirement is too high, then certainly the systems will not be

46
00:06:03,603 --> 00:06:11,935
Affordable, and it will be difficult to make products for the masses where affordability is a major concern.

47
00:06:12,516 --> 00:06:24,493
So, we should aim to build systems to minimize resource requirements as well while trying to minimize cyber sickness.

48
00:06:25,956 --> 00:06:32,445
Now, while trying to minimize resource requirements, we should not compromise on experience.

49
00:06:33,809 --> 00:06:44,120
We should try to maximize the immersive experience; then only we can say that this resource optimization is effective.

50
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:51,387
Otherwise, we may optimize resources, but if that affects the experience, then we cannot call it optimization.

51
00:06:52,568 --> 00:07:01,858
So, our goal is to build and deploy VR systems with as few resources as possible,

52
00:07:03,121 --> 00:07:12,898
without compromising on experience or without reducing the quality of experience to the extent possible.

53
00:07:13,039 --> 00:07:18,811
Of course, when we are trying to optimize two things, one is resource requirements and the other one is experience,

54
00:07:18,811 --> 00:07:27,745
we have to balance some trade-offs, and balancing those trade-offs is the optimization that we are talking about here.

55
00:07:30,644 --> 00:07:35,231
So, in this lecture, we will talk about ways to optimize experience.

56
00:07:35,271 --> 00:07:37,815
We will not focus on the resource optimization part here.

57
00:07:38,696 --> 00:07:47,308
In order to understand resource optimization, we require a detailed discussion on certain relevant things, which we will take up in subsequent lectures.

58
00:07:48,370 --> 00:07:52,496
This lecture will focus on experience optimization.

59
00:07:53,057 --> 00:08:00,027
Now, as we have already talked, when we talk of experience optimization, particularly in the context of VR,

60
00:08:00,395 --> 00:08:04,900
we are essentially referring to the fact that we are trying to minimize cyber sickness.

61
00:08:08,244 --> 00:08:13,510
So, experience optimization essentially can be equated with optimizing cyber sickness.

62
00:08:16,714 --> 00:08:24,343
Before we delve deeper into the experience optimization techniques, let us quickly recap what cyber sickness is.

63
00:08:25,488 --> 00:08:30,643
As you may recollect, it is also known as visually induced motion sickness, or VIMS,

64
00:08:30,643 --> 00:08:41,613
which is a condition that can affect individuals when they interact with virtual reality technologies and can be caused by a variety of factors.

65
00:08:43,416 --> 00:08:53,892
So, it is not a single-factor effect; a variety of factors can affect the cyber sickness in a user.

66
00:08:55,964 --> 00:08:57,846
So, what are those causes of cyber sickness?

67
00:08:59,747 --> 00:09:09,616
So, there are broadly two categories of factors we can relate to the induction of cyber sickness: internal factors and external factors.

68
00:09:09,656 --> 00:09:17,343
External factors can broadly be divided into hardware and VR content categories for cyber sickness.

69
00:09:17,764 --> 00:09:24,570
Now, under internal factors, we have several factors such as the age of the user,

70
00:09:24,989 --> 00:09:37,887
the gender of the user, susceptibility to motion sickness of the user, the physical condition of the user, and the emotional condition also of the user.

71
00:09:38,367 --> 00:09:51,325
So, these five are primarily responsible for cyber sickness in a user, which can be categorized under internal factors, internal to the user.

72
00:09:51,826 --> 00:09:54,169
Now, among the external factors, we have hardware,

73
00:09:54,689 --> 00:10:05,984
under which there is display resolution, latency, latency jitter, type of display, and type of input, which are primarily considered to be responsible for cyber sickness.

74
00:10:06,805 --> 00:10:22,485
Under VR content, we have visual motion, means of locomotion, field of view, type of content, duration, and visual complexity, which are responsible for cyber sickness.

75
00:10:24,625 --> 00:10:30,334
Of course, for details of each of these factors, you can refer to our earlier discussion on cyber sickness.

76
00:10:30,374 --> 00:10:32,557
We had a very detailed discussion on cyber sickness.

77
00:10:33,999 --> 00:10:35,582
Please refer to those.

78
00:10:37,485 --> 00:10:46,979
Now, finding a feasible solution to reduce the impact of cyber sickness on a user is extremely important for two reasons.

79
00:10:48,301 --> 00:10:52,287
It is supposed to greatly enhance overall user experience and

80
00:10:52,739 --> 00:10:56,464
make VR more appealing to a wider range of people.

81
00:10:57,045 --> 00:11:10,444
So, it is very important to find solutions to address the cyber sickness issue while the user is experiencing a VR system.

82
00:11:14,189 --> 00:11:20,738
Now, solutions to address cyber sickness issues

83
00:11:22,372 --> 00:11:26,378
broadly come under the mitigation strategies for mitigation of cyber sickness.

84
00:11:28,020 --> 00:11:40,385
Now, these mitigation strategies often come at the cost of presence; that is, if we try to reduce cyber sickness, then the presence may be

85
00:11:40,385 --> 00:11:49,711
reduced. That is, the user will feel more like in an artificial environment than a real environment.

86
00:11:49,731 --> 00:11:50,472
So, the user will

87
00:11:52,460 --> 00:11:55,503
be less likely to get a proper immersive experience.

88
00:11:57,345 --> 00:12:04,734
Now, this is where the challenge lies: developing mitigation strategies without compromising on user experience.

89
00:12:04,754 --> 00:12:16,847
So, our motto is that we should try to develop cyber sickness mitigation strategies, but at the same time, we should not compromise on presence,

90
00:12:19,173 --> 00:12:27,622
which indirectly is related to user experience.

91
00:12:27,642 --> 00:12:43,159
So, what we are going to learn in this lecture is we will try to understand how to minimize cyber sickness by minimizing the duration or distance covered in navigation.

92
00:12:43,179 --> 00:12:45,602
So, what we are going to learn is basically

93
00:12:46,763 --> 00:13:03,521
minimizing cyber sickness by minimizing the duration of navigation in the virtual environment or minimizing distance in navigation during the virtual experience.

94
00:13:03,541 --> 00:13:14,492
Now, while we are trying to minimize either of these, duration or distance covered, we should keep in mind that we should not compromise on presence.

95
00:13:14,742 --> 00:13:25,817
So, our objective is to try to minimize duration or distance in navigation while trying to maximize presence.

96
00:13:31,145 --> 00:13:35,611
So, then it brings us to the issue of VR navigation.

97
00:13:35,651 --> 00:13:42,420
So, we are trying to optimize user experience.

98
00:13:43,278 --> 00:13:51,191
In immersive systems, in order to do so, we are trying to minimize cyber sickness without compromising on presence.

99
00:13:51,211 --> 00:13:59,982
And we are trying to minimize cyber sickness by minimizing the duration of exposure to the virtual world or,

100
00:13:59,982 --> 00:14:08,020
by minimizing the distance covered in the virtual world during navigation in the virtual world.

101
00:14:09,198 --> 00:14:12,101
While doing so, we try to maximize presence.

102
00:14:12,121 --> 00:14:20,411
Now, since we are talking of navigation, the duration or distance covered in navigation, let us quickly have a recap on VR navigation.

103
00:14:21,472 --> 00:14:25,376
We have already had a detailed discussion on VR navigation earlier.

104
00:14:25,717 --> 00:14:27,479
Let us just have a quick recap.

105
00:14:32,765 --> 00:14:37,450
To navigate in the VR world, we use virtual locomotion techniques or

106
00:14:38,071 --> 00:14:39,312
In short, VLTs.

107
00:14:40,333 --> 00:14:48,461
Now, when you talk of VLTs, there are different categories of virtual locomotion techniques, as we have discussed earlier in detail.

108
00:14:50,743 --> 00:15:02,995
We have walking-based locomotion techniques where physical walking is involved, under which we can have full gait, partial gait, and gait negation techniques.

109
00:15:03,535 --> 00:15:05,277
Then we have steering-based locomotion techniques,

110
00:15:07,484 --> 00:15:15,255
selection-based locomotion techniques, and finally, manipulation-based locomotion techniques.

111
00:15:15,275 --> 00:15:24,228
So, broadly, these four categories of techniques are utilized for locomotion in the virtual world, that is, moving from one point to another.

112
00:15:28,674 --> 00:15:32,800
Now, VLTs allow us to perform navigation in the VR world.

113
00:15:33,522 --> 00:15:43,551
In other words, we can say that VLTs allow us to perform a VR tour, or virtual reality tour, in the virtual environment.

114
00:15:43,571 --> 00:15:51,679
Now, these virtual reality tours, based on the way they are made, can be categorized into two broad groups.

115
00:15:54,262 --> 00:15:57,505
One is user-controlled virtual tours.

116
00:15:57,525 --> 00:16:01,048
The second one is system-controlled virtual tours.

117
00:16:03,762 --> 00:16:09,747
In the case of user-controlled virtual tours, what do we perform?

118
00:16:10,708 --> 00:16:20,236
So, user-controlled tours essentially refer to a tour in which the user has control over their travel and navigation within the VR environment.

119
00:16:20,256 --> 00:16:30,725
So, the user can control how to navigate, where to navigate; the user has full control of their navigation in the virtual world. That is a user-controlled VR tour.

120
00:16:32,507 --> 00:16:33,668
It typically involves

121
00:16:34,407 --> 00:16:43,783
controllers or other input modalities to move and explore virtual space according to the preferences and actions of the user.

122
00:16:43,803 --> 00:16:46,808
So, some means of interaction must be there.

123
00:16:50,014 --> 00:16:53,640
As opposed to that, we have system-controlled virtual tours.

124
00:16:53,780 --> 00:16:58,508
Here, the system or application is

125
00:16:58,943 --> 00:17:02,648
in control of the user's travel within the VR environment.

126
00:17:02,688 --> 00:17:08,841
So, in the case of a user-controlled tour, the user is controlling how to move and where to move, whereas in the case of a system-controlled tour,

127
00:17:08,841 --> 00:17:15,483
the system is controlling how the user is moving in the virtual world and where the user is moving.

128
00:17:21,310 --> 00:17:28,140
So, the user's movement and navigation are guided or controlled by the system itself.

129
00:17:28,778 --> 00:17:36,310
Unlike in the previous case, where the user is controlling the movement and navigation.

130
00:17:39,655 --> 00:17:50,852
Now, since the system is controlling the user's movement, such techniques or such VR tools often follow a predetermined path or sequence of movements.

131
00:17:52,375 --> 00:17:55,680
It is already decided which way to move and where to move.

132
00:17:57,483 --> 00:18:03,853
Now, like any other system, we have positives and negatives for system-controlled tools as well.

133
00:18:04,775 --> 00:18:17,433
On the positive side, we have this positive effect: that is, system-controlled tools are often an effortless experience with reduced cognitive demands,

134
00:18:17,433 --> 00:18:21,482
allowing the users to concentrate more on the experience itself.

135
00:18:22,205 --> 00:18:24,588
Why is the cognitive demand reduced?

136
00:18:24,929 --> 00:18:28,935
The user does not have to decide which way to move, how to move, or any of those things.

137
00:18:28,975 --> 00:18:31,398
The system takes care of those decision-making things.

138
00:18:33,321 --> 00:18:49,444
So the user is free to choose to experience, to concentrate more on the experience itself rather than spend time on planning to move.

139
00:18:52,698 --> 00:18:54,319
On the negative side, what is there?

140
00:18:54,760 --> 00:18:58,383
Lack of user involvement makes them less realistic.

141
00:18:58,423 --> 00:19:04,949
So since the user is unable to control the movement, the user feels like it is not real.

142
00:19:05,349 --> 00:19:11,855
It is a system in which the user is experiencing the thing which defeats the very purpose of presence.

143
00:19:20,643 --> 00:19:21,844
Whatever that is,

144
00:19:22,128 --> 00:19:24,211
whatever positive and negative sides it has.

145
00:19:24,792 --> 00:19:30,240
So, in this lecture, we will be focusing on system-controlled tours.

146
00:19:30,260 --> 00:19:37,190
More specifically, we will be focusing on realistic walking experiences in system-controlled tours.

147
00:19:37,210 --> 00:19:42,718
So, while the navigation is taking place in a system-controlled tour, the user should not feel like

148
00:19:43,610 --> 00:19:46,534
they are actually being controlled by the system.

149
00:19:46,554 --> 00:19:51,643
Instead, if the walking experience or navigation experience is realistic,

150
00:19:51,643 --> 00:20:03,979
the user will forget, or the difference between the realistic and unrealistic things will vanish, will reduce in the mind of the user.

151
00:20:04,460 --> 00:20:05,922
So, our objective is to

152
00:20:08,602 --> 00:20:18,531
have a realistic walking experience in a system control tool so that the user feels present in the tool.

153
00:20:18,631 --> 00:20:35,086
Subsequently, we will see how that realistic walking experience approach that we can adopt to make the user feel present in the tool can be utilized to optimize user experience.

154
00:20:35,106 --> 00:20:37,748
So, why are we focusing on a realistic walking experience?

155
00:20:39,483 --> 00:20:51,178
Now, improving the walking experience in VR tours is vital for enhanced realism, or in other words, enhanced presence.

156
00:20:54,682 --> 00:20:59,649
For that, it is essential to use the user's actual walking speed as the travel speed.

157
00:21:00,029 --> 00:21:09,321
So, when the user is navigating in a system-controlled tour, that is, the system is forcing the user to move from one place to another, the speed at which the user is moving

158
00:21:10,516 --> 00:21:13,300
should match the user's real-world experience.

159
00:21:14,501 --> 00:21:20,089
Now, here we are assuming that the navigation is taking place as if the user is walking.

160
00:21:20,830 --> 00:21:23,053
Of course, that is not necessarily the case always.

161
00:21:23,714 --> 00:21:40,236
However, in our case, we are assuming that the user is navigating through walking, and it is essential to use the user's actual walking speed to give the user a feel of realism.

162
00:21:43,895 --> 00:21:52,264
Now, when you say 'actual,' it refers to the user's typical walking behavior, not during walking in a VR tour.

163
00:21:55,067 --> 00:22:02,335
So, the challenge here is that system-automated tours typically do not allow for continuous user input.

164
00:22:02,355 --> 00:22:06,640
So, that makes it difficult to align the user's input with the walking speed.

165
00:22:06,660 --> 00:22:13,007
So, if the input mechanism is not there, then how do we tell

166
00:22:13,561 --> 00:22:27,882
or how the user can tell whether they want to move at a natural walking speed or whether the speed being provided by the system is good enough.

167
00:22:27,902 --> 00:22:35,613
So, one way out can be a constant walking speed as an optic flow, or in other words, the speed of change of the scene.

168
00:22:35,933 --> 00:22:38,197
So, how do we perceive that somebody is moving?

169
00:22:40,840 --> 00:22:42,623
We perceive our movement by

170
00:22:44,054 --> 00:22:47,737
perceiving the change in our view of the surroundings.

171
00:22:47,978 --> 00:22:49,499
In other words, change in the scene.

172
00:22:50,740 --> 00:22:54,924
Now, in a virtual world, a virtual tour, the same thing happens.

173
00:22:55,184 --> 00:22:59,308
When I say the user is moving or navigating, that means the scene is changing constantly.

174
00:22:59,769 --> 00:23:06,815
Now, the speed at which the scene changes needs to match the user's realistic walking speed.

175
00:23:07,356 --> 00:23:09,138
Otherwise, it will look unrealistic.

176
00:23:09,158 --> 00:23:10,759
It may be very fast or very slow.

177
00:23:13,582 --> 00:23:13,842
Now,

178
00:23:14,986 --> 00:23:29,800
One way to make it look more realistic is to have a constant walking speed as an optic flow; that is, the speed at which the scenes change remains constant over time.

179
00:23:32,483 --> 00:23:42,613
But this is not very realistic, of course, as we can intuitively understand, because when we move, we rarely follow a constant speed while walking.

180
00:23:43,876 --> 00:23:45,198
We ourselves do not do it.

181
00:23:45,498 --> 00:23:53,908
So, if the system is doing so, there is less possibility that we will feel realistic while exploring the virtual space.

182
00:23:56,912 --> 00:24:01,678
Now, our objective is to have a more realistic experience.

183
00:24:03,780 --> 00:24:13,512
In other words, we are trying to display a visual speed or optic flow that matches the user's instantaneous walking speed.

184
00:24:15,483 --> 00:24:22,373
So, essentially, what we are trying to figure out is a model of the user's walking speed.

185
00:24:23,775 --> 00:24:38,617
How typically a user moves and what is the instantaneous speed during movement, and accordingly, we should try to change the scenario, the visual scene in the virtual world.

186
00:24:39,258 --> 00:24:42,503
Then it may give the user a realistic feeling.

187
00:24:47,562 --> 00:24:53,025
So, in the next part of the lecture, we are going to learn about such a model which can provide

188
00:24:53,025 --> 00:25:00,080
an idea of the instantaneous walking speed, and correspondingly, we can have a more realistic walking experience.

189
00:25:00,100 --> 00:25:04,066
However, that we will take up in the next part of the lecture.

190
00:25:07,130 --> 00:25:09,673
So, let us summarize what we have covered so far.

191
00:25:09,854 --> 00:25:15,662
We talked about the idea of optimization in VR; there we talked about two optimizations:

192
00:25:16,385 --> 00:25:18,247
psychophysiological and technological.

193
00:25:18,287 --> 00:25:32,141
Now, the psychophysiological optimization, we essentially equated it with the VR experience optimization.

194
00:25:32,161 --> 00:25:44,434
Further, we equated it with optimizing cyber sickness prevalence; that is, we want to minimize cyber sickness while the user is experiencing the immersive system.

195
00:25:45,595 --> 00:25:49,139
So, that is the way we have framed the optimization problem.

196
00:25:49,740 --> 00:25:51,402
How do we minimize cyber sickness?

197
00:25:52,183 --> 00:26:03,744
And in order to do so, we assume that we are talking about system-controlled VR tools where the user is navigating through walking and

198
00:26:03,744 --> 00:26:12,227
we want to give the user a realistic walking experience so that the user feels present in the system.

199
00:26:13,355 --> 00:26:16,699
When we try to optimize cyber sickness, of course, presence is very important.

200
00:26:16,840 --> 00:26:27,273
If we compromise on presence, then whether you optimize cyber sickness or not, essentially the quality of experience will deteriorate.

201
00:26:27,293 --> 00:26:30,838
So, taking care of presence is very important.

202
00:26:31,679 --> 00:26:41,151
Towards that direction, we are trying to have a natural walking experience in the VR world, in a system-controlled VR tour.

203
00:26:44,708 --> 00:26:54,781
In the next part of the lecture, we are going to talk about models for optimization which include a description of a model for natural walking experience.

204
00:26:56,944 --> 00:27:01,069
I hope you could follow the topic, you could follow the concepts.

